A||||6||gabriel||Seat problem||||1||
Z||000000||gabriel||02-23-2001||10:44 PM||||Hi all.<P>Well, I'm trying to replace the carpet, but the passenger seat has a problem.  I swear, the prior owner did zero maintenence. <P>  Okay, the rear bolt nearest the door  was coming off nicely when the stud must have broken its weld connecting it to the pan.<P>  Now the whole thing (nut & stud) just turns.<P>  Is there any way to remove the seat from the track without removing it from the car?<P>  Otherwise, the only way I can see to remove this would be to attach a small vise grips to the nut, remove the seat cover and drill right through the seat with an extension bit to reach the stud and drill it out.<P>  Also, how IS this stud attached?  I'll have to figure out a replacement setup.<BR> Any thoughts on this?  (Zebras welcome)||||reg||1
Z||000001||2xsess||02-23-2001||11:48 PM||||The only way you could remove the seat fromt he track while it's still installed in the car would be to take the 4 bolts that hold it to the track, I'm not even sure if it's possible. <P>My suggestion would be, spray the nut with a good penetrating oil and see if you can get a pair of needle nose vise-grips on the stud and take the nut off. Drilling through the seat seems like an extreme measure to me.<P>Just my $.02||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||02-24-2001||12:09 AM||||go to Sears or some other place that has tools and buy a nut cracker. It will split the nut off the stud. That will let you get the seat out.<P>YOU MUST fix that stud before putting the seat back in. ALL FOUR studs must be present and in use for the seat to be safe.<P>Trust me on this..... American car seats are weak enough without having missing hardware. At least in Fiero they can't collapse backward like they do in most cars. ||||reg||2
Z||000003||Tigger||02-24-2001||12:41 AM||||This happened to me too.        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>A nut breaker is the best thing to break the nut off.  I didn't have one when I fixed mine, so I used a dremel to grind down a slot on both sides of the nut and then used a chisel and hammer to break the nut.  It was a pain.<P>I used an arc welder to weld the stud back in.<UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>First, don't remove the stud or cut it off.<BR><LI>Grind away the paint/rust around the stud.<BR><LI>drilled three 1/8" or so 3/32" holes just around the stud, 120 degrees apart.<BR><LI>Then hold up on the stud with some vice grips and weld in around the stud and holes to fill them in and get a solid weld.<BR></UL><P>You'll need welding experience for sure...<BR>Overall, I was happy with the results.  Didn't have to cut a hole in the floorpan or cut up the crossmember and seat track.  The stud felt like it was held in solid, better than the production spot welds too.<P>Here's a quick picture of the stud and repair:<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seatstud_1.gif"> <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 02-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hank is Here||02-24-2001||01:13 AM||||Ouch a nut cracker sounds painful!  But seriously a nut cracker or splitter works wonders.  There are two versions I have used<P>1) the more heavy duty (IMOHP) is one that works like scissors but opposite(or internal snap ring pliers) .  It has two differnet arms, a bolt is forced agins the othe arm causing the pressure to split or <P>2) follows the same basic principal.  It is a circle with a bolt with a sharp end entering the circle with a point at the other end.  Screwing in the bolt tightens the nut and splits it.  <P>the advantage to #1 is that the splitting can be done from more than one angle.  I believe what is at Sears is the second type, but I could be wrong.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||gabriel||02-24-2001||07:23 AM||||Thanks guys.<P>  Ogre: You are right about the safety concerns, thanks.<P>  Tigger:  Thanks for the great diagram & all.<P>  I had gone to a local parts stote & they wanted $53.00+ for a splitter!!!<P>  Like, I didn,t want to to make their car payment, I just wanted a little tool!<P>  I'll head to Sears & break out the welder.  Again, Thanks guys.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wolfhound||02-24-2001||07:35 AM||||Another possibility is using a Dremel tool and abrasive disk to cut a screw slot in the top of the Stud to hold while you remove the nut. I,ve done this in similar situations.||||reg||1
